    "titre2": "Exercise 6",
    "texte": [
      "Narrative & Literary Reflection — At half past six on a rainy Tuesday evening, thousands of commuters pour out of the central underground station into the neon-lit canyons of the metropolis, their shoulders brushing in a dense choreography of hurried anonymity. Within a radius of a single square kilometer, tens of thousands of people eat dinner behind illuminated apartment windows, scroll through glowing screens on cramped buses, or sit alone at corner café tables surrounded by the murmur of strangers. Few spectacles capture the paradox of contemporary urban life more poignantly than this collision between extreme physical proximity and profound emotional solitude: never in human history have so many people lived so close together while feeling so isolated from one another.",
      "For centuries, sociologists and novelists alike have observed that the modern metropolis offers an intoxicating liberation from the intrusive surveillance of small-town life. In the city, individuals are free to reinvent themselves, escape stifling familial expectations, and seek out niche communities tailored to their intellectual or artistic sensibilities. Yet this very anonymity—so prized when it protects personal privacy—becomes corrosive when it hardens into mutual indifference. Built around transient professional networks, high residential turnover, and the commodification of leisure, the contemporary megacity often strips away the unplanned, everyday encounters that once wove neighbors into a supportive social fabric.",
      "Furthermore, the architectural and economic evolution of global cities has inadvertently deepened this quiet epidemic of disconnection. Soaring real estate prices push young workers, families, and elderly residents toward sprawling peripheral suburbs, lengthening daily commutes and leaving little time or energy for civic clubs, neighborhood associations, or casual conversation. Meanwhile, within affluent urban cores, fortified luxury towers, private delivery services, and remote work allow residents to satisfy every material need without ever crossing paths with a shopkeeper, a neighbor, or a fellow citizen. Convenience is maximized, yet the friction of shared public life—the very friction through which empathy and trust are cultivated—is systematically engineered out of daily routines.",
      "Epidemiologists and psychologists now warn that chronic urban loneliness is far more than a private melancholy; it is a major public health crisis whose physiological toll rivals that of smoking or sedentary living. Persistent social isolation elevates stress hormones, weakens immune resilience, and erodes cognitive vitality, affecting not only elderly residents living alone after bereavement, but also young professionals newly arrived in unfamiliar cities and students navigating hyper-competitive universities. Because loneliness still carries a subtle social stigma—often misread as a personal failure of sociability—many sufferers conceal their isolation behind curated digital performances of busyness.",
      "Addressing loneliness at the heart of the metropolis therefore requires far more than urging isolated individuals to download networking applications. It calls for reimagining urban design and civic culture around human connection: investing in accessible libraries, parks, community kitchens, and mixed-income housing; designing public spaces that invite lingering rather than mere transit; and reviving local rituals of mutual aid. Only when cities are built not merely as engines of economic efficiency, but as hospitable habitats for shared human presence, can the crowd cease to be a lonely place."
