      "Continuation of letter to Julian — Elena's account illustrates what labor economists term 'asymmetrical flexibility,' wherein employers capture the benefits of an on-demand workforce without bearing the traditional obligations of social protection. Yet precariousness is by no means confined to digital platforms; it equally pervades logistics, hospitality, retail, and care sectors.",
      "Testimony from Samuel, 48, warehouse logistics associate on a zero-hours contract: 'Every Sunday evening, I wait anxiously by my phone to learn how many shifts I have been allocated for the coming week. Sometimes I am scheduled for forty-five hours; other weeks, barely twelve. That unpredictability makes it nearly impossible to budget for family expenses, pursue evening training courses, or qualify for a standard housing lease. Worse still, speaking up about unsafe working conditions carries the unspoken threat of seeing one's shifts quietly vanish the following week.'",
      "Closing of letter to Julian — As both Elena and Samuel demonstrate, employment precariousness corrodes far more than household budgets: it undermines long-term life planning, mental health, and workplace voice. Reversing this trend does not require outlawing genuinely flexible work, but rather decoupling basic social protections—healthcare, retirement, training, and income insurance—from traditional full-time contracts, while enforcing transparent algorithmic governance and collective representation for all workers."
