    "texte": [
      "Public Speech — Ladies and gentlemen, esteemed colleagues, few questions in political economy provoke sharper ideological division than how societies should distribute the wealth generated by collective human ingenuity. Over the past four decades, rapid financial globalization, technological automation, and shifts in tax policy have coincided with an extraordinary concentration of private assets at the very top of the economic pyramid. While aggregate global output has expanded substantially, vast segments of the working and middle classes across both affluent and developing nations face stagnating real wages, soaring housing and education costs, and heightened economic insecurity. Addressing twenty-first-century crises—from climate transition to demographic aging—requires confronting the mechanisms of wealth redistribution with renewed intellectual rigor.",
      "Critics of ambitious redistribution frequently invoke the classic metaphor of the economic pie, warning that progressive taxation and expansive social transfers blunt entrepreneurial incentives, discourage capital investment, and trigger capital flight across borders. According to this trickle-down orthodoxy, maximizing aggregate growth inevitably lifts all boats over time, rendering deliberate equalization counterproductive. Yet empirical evidence from the past half-century casts serious doubt on that assumption. Far from accelerating shared prosperity, extreme wealth concentration often depresses aggregate consumer demand—since high-income households save a far larger fraction of their earnings than working families—while enabling affluent elites to exert disproportionate influence over legislative agendas and regulatory frameworks.",
      "Moreover, contemporary wealth accumulation rarely reflects purely isolated individual effort. Modern fortunes rely heavily on publicly funded foundational research, digital and transport infrastructure, educated workforces, legal protections of intellectual property, and stable financial institutions sustained by society as a whole. Recognizing the profoundly social nature of wealth creation reframes redistribution not as punitive confiscation of private merit, but as a fair return on collective public investment and an essential safeguard of democratic equality.",
      "Effective redistribution in the modern era must nevertheless evolve beyond twentieth-century national templates to address new structural realities. Closing offshore tax havens through coordinated international minimum corporate and wealth taxes can curb the race to the bottom that erodes public revenues. Simultaneously, policymakers must balance traditional ex-post redistribution—such as progressive income taxes and targeted social transfers—with 'predistribution' strategies that equalize opportunities before market outcomes diverge: universal early-childhood education, affordable healthcare, accessible vocational training, and broader worker ownership stakes in emerging industries.",
      "Ultimately, wealth redistribution is not merely a technical accounting exercise; it is a foundational choice about the kind of society we wish to inhabit. Extreme inequality corrodes social trust, fuels political polarization, and undermines the collective solidarity required to finance ecological transition and public health. By designing fair, transparent, and globally coordinated mechanisms to share prosperity broadly, democracies can reconcile dynamic innovation with human dignity and lasting civic stability."
