      "Dr. Sofia Alvarez: 'That question cuts to the heart of contemporary global governance. True economic sovereignty does not mean autarkic isolation from world markets; rather, it signifies a state's capacity to mobilize domestic resources, design coherent industrial and social policies, and negotiate international agreements on equitable terms. Yet for many low- and middle-income countries, structural constraints severely narrow that policy space. Borrowing predominantly in foreign currencies exposes national budgets to exchange-rate shocks and interest-rate hikes decided in distant financial centers, often forcing governments to slash public healthcare and education precisely when citizens need support most.'",
      "Dr. Kwame Boateng: 'Trade and investment rules frequently compound those financial pressures. Under asymmetrical bilateral investment treaties and rigid intellectual property regimes, developing countries can face costly international arbitration simply for enacting environmental safeguards, regulating pharmaceutical prices, or requiring foreign firms to transfer technology and source inputs locally. Meanwhile, illicit financial flows and aggressive corporate tax avoidance drain tens of billions of dollars annually from the very economies that urgently need domestic tax revenue to build infrastructure.'",
      "Dr. Sofia Alvarez: 'And yet, blaming external rules alone would overlook the decisive role of domestic governance and regional cooperation. Developing nations expand their economic sovereignty dramatically when they strengthen progressive domestic taxation, combat corruption, invest in scientific research and technical education, and deepen regional economic integration. Look at how continental free-trade areas and regional development banks enable smaller economies to pool bargaining power, build cross-border value chains, and reduce dependence on single-commodity exports.'",
