      "Historians of technology point out that reliance on tools is hardly new; humanity has always co-evolved with its inventions, from agriculture and metallurgy to electricity and telecommunications. What distinguishes the current era, however, is the speed, opacity, and extreme centralization of our technological dependencies. Whereas earlier mechanical systems were often locally repairable and intelligible to skilled artisans, modern digital ecosystems rely on layered cloud architectures, proprietary algorithms, and global semiconductor supply chains controlled by a handful of dominant corporations. When a single point of failure occurs—whether through human error, extreme weather, or a coordinated cyberattack—cascading disruptions can paralyze entire regions within minutes.",
