"Public Speech — Ladies and gentlemen, members of the Academy, honored guests, few convictions have propelled human civilization forward more powerfully than the pursuit of scientific inquiry. From understanding the laws of thermodynamics and microbiology to unlocking quantum mechanics and artificial intelligence, science has repeatedly expanded human capability and alleviated ancient forms of suffering. Yet across modern history, a recurring temptation has accompanied this triumph: the belief that scientific knowledge is morally neutral, and that anything technically feasible ought inevitably to be pursued. When humanity acquires the capacity to split the atom, alter ecosystems, or engineer pathogens, where does legitimate scientific exploration end and grave moral responsibility begin?",
"For centuries, defenders of unqualified scientific autonomy invoked the ideal of 'pure research,' arguing that scientists merely uncover objective facts about nature while politicians, corporations, or citizens bear sole responsibility for how those discoveries are applied. However, in the twenty-first century, the boundary between fundamental discovery and technological deployment has grown vanishingly thin. In fields such as synthetic biology, autonomous weapons systems, and neurotechnology, the very design of an experiment can carry immediate dual-use consequences—capable of curing disease or enabling unprecedented harm. Scientists can no longer shelter behind a posture of detached neutrality when their work directly reshapes the conditions of human survival.",
"At the same time, moral responsibility in science cannot be reduced to a simplistic demand to halt research whenever uncertainty arises. History demonstrates that excessive fear or dogmatic censorship can stifle discoveries that later save millions of lives, from vaccines to clean energy technologies. Genuine ethical responsibility is therefore not the enemy of scientific curiosity; rather, it is a disciplined commitment to foresight, transparency, and proportionality. It requires asking not merely 'Can we build this?' but 'Who benefits, who bears the risks, what irreversible harms might ensue, and have those affected been consulted?'",
